## Phase 1: Establish the native addition contract
    Outcome: document the native APIs, call sequence and implementation guards. Implementation and live rejection/restoration validation belong to Phases 3 and 4; this investigation does not claim a working picker or atomic server acceptance.
    Scope correction (2026-10-07): the original phase unnecessarily required implementation-level recovery proof before implementation. That verification is retained in Phase 4, where the actual picker can be tested. The existing-tool-mode/no-new-server-handler requirement is unchanged.
    ☑ Inspect ItemChisel.GetToolModes/SetToolMode and BEChisel.AddMaterial for material identity, consumption, full/invalid/creative cases, composite stacks, dirty marking and keep-open behavior.
    ☑ Identify the native one-item transfer API and its ownership, capacity and packet-return requirements.
    ☑ Specify the caller sequence and guards using native inventory operations and the existing ToolModeSelection.Apply.
    ☑ Record native correction limits and the cases that must be verified against the implemented picker in Phase 4.
    ☑ Perform a second source review and independent completion audit of this investigation contract.
    Completion criteria:
        ☑ The source-backed contract names the owning APIs and gives an actionable sequence without new server handling or manual stack assignment.
        ☑ Source conclusions are distinguished from runtime validation; all runtime acceptance requirements remain in Phase 4.

    Native operation contract:
        1. Resolve addmat by code from fresh vanilla modes. Validate the captured chisel and target, current selected source stack, eligible material and empty MouseItemSlot. Resolve source slots from the player's owned hotbar/backpack inventories; include eligible offhand storage and exclude bag equipment slots/external containers.
        2. Create ItemStackMoveOperation with RequestedQuantity = 1, Left mouse button, no modifiers and DirectMerge. Call InventoryManager.TryTransferTo(source, MouseItemSlot, ref operation). Require a returned packet and exactly one moved item before proceeding; send that existing inventory packet first. CanTake/CanTakeFrom, storage flags, capacity and CanContain remain native-owned.
        3. Call ToolModeSelection.Apply with the current addmat index and cloned opening BlockSelection. Apply calls native SetToolMode, sends existing tool-mode packet 27 and marks the chisel slot dirty.
        4. Return a locally unconsumed staged item using TryTransferTo(MouseItemSlot, originalSource, ref operation), then send the returned native inventory packet. Validate the current source and cursor contents before returning; stop on transfer failure. Do not overwrite a changed cursor/source, manufacture a replacement stack or guess a retry.
        ItemChisel.SetToolMode prioritizes a block on MouseItemSlot over the supplied mode index. Ordinary survival consumes one item when the previous material supply is not full. Creative, invalid and already-full ordinary materials retain it. The normal local call order is therefore inventory transfer, tool-mode application, then inventory return if needed; send packets in that order.
        Valid material addition marks the target dirty and emits keepopentoolmodedlg; survival consumption also marks MouseItemSlot dirty. MarkDirty does not replace sending the native client packet.
        Composite BlockChisel items add their stored constituent quantities and consume one in survival when a materials array exists, irrespective of individual full flags. Validate matching arrays, resolved materials and positive contributions before invoking vanilla; missing targets or malformed arrays can throw.
        BEChisel.AddMaterial mutates quantities even when it reports an ordinary material already full. It is not a capacity-preview API. Exclude noncontributing/full choices through read-only candidate checks, while preserving native execution semantics.
        Resolve addmat again when rebuilding the parent menu because its index changes with the target material list. When the cursor is empty, SetToolMode follows its ordinary mode/material-selection branch; do not invoke it after failed local staging.

    Evidence and limits:
        Material behavior -> ../../vssurvivalmod/Item/ItemChisel.cs GetToolModes, IsValidChiselingMaterial and SetToolMode; ../../vssurvivalmod/Systems/Microblock/BEChisel.cs AddMaterial.
        Transfer ownership -> ../../vsapi/Common/Entity/Player/IPlayerInventoryManager.cs TryTransferTo; ../../vsapi/Common/Inventory/ItemSlot.cs CanTakeFrom/CanHold/TryPutInto/MarkDirty; QuickToolInventoryView/QuickToolMovementPlan demonstrate current owned-slot/native-packet conventions.
        Tool application -> ../VanillaExpanded/src/ToolModeRadialMenu/ToolModeSelection.cs Apply/CreatePacket and ToolModeRadialMenuSystem.cs captured target; ChiselToolModeMenuLayoutStrategy.cs identifies addmat by code.
        Source inspection establishes these APIs and branches. Prior installed-engine inspection of version 1.22.7.0 found that inventory and tool-mode packets are separate operations, native revision checks can reject inventory movement, and the tool-mode operation has no transaction acknowledgement. Those observations are limits, not a claim of validated picker recovery.
        Local consumption, SlotModified and an empty predicted cursor do not prove server acceptance. Use existing authoritative synchronization; do not introduce an acknowledgement protocol or an inventory-tracking framework. Server-only rejection can leave a cursor remainder that local prediction did not retain. This must be exercised with the implemented picker in Phase 4.
        Closing the last dialog can drop a visible cursor remainder when mouse grab resumes; closing is not source restoration. Finish the synchronous local return before normal closure where possible, and leave disconnect/correction to native authority rather than sending guessed operations after leaving the world.
        No reverted probe sources/results are claimed as current validation. No source/test changes are needed to complete this documentation investigation.

    Review receipt (2026-10-07): second source review and independent completion audit passed for this documentation contract. Runtime acceptance remains unchecked below.

## Phase 2: Build eligible material content
    Outcome: the picker represents every supported eligible source stack with readable native item icons.
    ☐ Add a small chisel-owned candidate enumerator using current player-owned inventories and stable slot addresses.
        ☐ Require a block-bearing, nonempty stack and use the native IsValidChiselingMaterial rules at the captured target.
        ☐ Investigate the validator's in-game error side effects and conditional interfaces; avoid emitting rejection messages while scanning an inventory. Document any necessary quiet validation boundary without silently changing eligibility.
        ☐ Preserve composite chiseled-block attributes and exclude malformed/noncontributing compositions safely.
        ☐ Define target-capacity eligibility using the verified native behavior, including replenishment of existing materials; do not call AddMaterial to test eligibility or invent different consumption semantics.
    ☐ Build paged radial content with item name/count, native stack icon, Back, and page navigation.
        ☐ Keep candidate IDs distinct from numeric tool-mode IDs. Keep ordering deterministic across pages.
        ☐ Handle zero candidates with a clear message and Back; handle one candidate without accidentally replacing Back with a full-screen selection disc.
        ☐ Keep the existing ToolModeMenuSize setting and shared geometry limits. Confirm all candidates remain accessible beyond 63 entries.
    ☐ Verification: eligibility/config/conditional blocks, ordinary and composite stacks, offhand/backpack scope, zero/one/many choices, and page coverage.
    Completion criteria:
        ☐ Every eligible carried stack is reachable, with no external-container candidates or quiet truncation; enumeration neither moves items nor mutates the target.

## Phase 3: Integrate selection with the existing tool-mode operation
    Outcome: Add Material transitions to the picker and selecting a stack executes the verified native sequence.
    ☐ Intercept only the chisel addmat action before ordinary ToolModeSelection.Apply; enter the picker with UpdateLayout and KeepOpen.
    ☐ Keep the selection owner thin; place candidate/content building and the native material operation in chisel-specific responsibilities, without adding a general interaction framework.
    ☐ Before staging, revalidate the active chisel, captured target, current source slot/stack identity and relevant attributes, count, eligibility, and empty cursor.
        ☐ Refresh or decline stale candidates; do not consume a replacement stack that merely occupies the same slot.
        ☐ Resolve the current addmat index from fresh vanilla modes while retaining the captured target.
    ☐ Execute the Phase 1 sequence through native inventory APIs and existing ToolModeSelection.Apply. Prevent overlapping additions while native reconciliation is pending.
        ☐ Follow verified failure/restoration rules; never delete a remainder, overwrite another cursor stack, or report success solely from local prediction.
        ☐ Keep any operation lifetime independent of picker closure only as required to finish verified native restoration; clear world-local state on disconnect.
    ☐ Return Back to the parent menu without applying a tool mode. After the addition sequence settles, regenerate parent modes from current target data and preserve the current carving mode.
    ☐ Preserve ordinary tool-mode selection, chisel cursor-drop behavior outside the picker where applicable, and quick-swap menus.
    ☐ Verification: callback transitions, captured target/tool guards, one-item staging, exact existing packet sequence, restoration cases, repeated selection, cancellation, and refreshed modes.
    Completion criteria:
        ☐ The special action uses the existing native operation end to end, without new server code or changes to shared GUI input ownership.

## Phase 4: Validate and reconcile documentation
    Outcome: focused receipts and user-run game checks establish inventory conservation and usable material picking.
    ☐ Use a subagent for focused tests and the production Release build; perform a second review and independent completion audit before checking completion markers.
    ☐ Run focused tests for candidate enumeration, pagination, native operation/failure sequence, and existing tool-mode/radial behavior. Separate mocks from installed-native and server evidence.
    ☐ Verify the implemented native staging/application/return sequence, including packet send order, native accessibility/capacity and equipped-backpack persistence.
        ☐ Exercise server-only staging/addition/return rejection, a corrected cursor remainder, revision rejection and delayed updates. Establish supported failure/closure behavior before claiming acceptance; an empty predicted cursor is not proof of server consumption.
        ☐ Verify any necessary native backpack reload after authoritative correction through the owning inventory API. Do not manually reconstruct inventory state.
    ☐ Complete user-run singleplayer and multiplayer checks: hotbar/backpack/offhand sources, invalid/full materials, composite stacks, creative, ordinary survival consumption, repeated additions, and delayed server corrections.
        ☐ Check source/cursor/target state after cancellation and failure; verify no item duplication, loss, unintended tool-mode changes, or changes to a newly aimed block.
        ☐ Check readable icons/pages, zero-candidate feedback, Back, occupied-cursor feedback, application focus loss, and disconnect.
    ☐ Verify packaged output/assets and translated player-facing labels where required by the implementation.
    ☐ Reconcile project.todo to this plan and explicitly retire the old inventory-interaction plan as the active chisel solution; preserve history without claiming its reverted implementation is present.
    Completion criteria:
        ☐ Native packet ordering and multiplayer conservation have runtime evidence; headless tests are not presented as live acceptance.
        ☐ The delivered code remains chisel-specific and uses no new server packet/handler or shared input-routing system.
        ☐ All unresolved assumptions are either verified or explicitly approved as scoped limitations before completion.
